message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "Do Foundation Models Know Geometry? Probing Frozen Features for Continuous Physical Measurement"
authors:
  - family-names: Shkolnikov
    given-names: Yakov Pyotr
    email: yshkolni@gmail.com
type: article
date-released: "2026-02-26"
identifiers:
  - type: other
    value: "arXiv:2603.06459"
    description: "arXiv preprint"
url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.06459"
repository-code: "https://github.com/yshk-mxim/probing-frozen-features-continuous"
keywords:
  - foundation models
  - vision-language models
  - geometric probing
  - linear probes
  - hand pose estimation
abstract: >-
  Vision-language models encode continuous geometry that their text
  pathway fails to express: a 6,000-parameter linear probe extracts
  hand joint angles at 6.1 degree MAE from frozen features, while the
  best text output achieves only 20.0 degrees, a 3.3x bottleneck.
  LoRA fine-tuning (r=16, 2,000 images) narrows this gap to 6.5 degrees,
  providing evidence for a pathway-training deficit rather than a
  representational one. Training objective determines accuracy more than
  architecture: five encoders spanning self-supervised, contrastive, and
  hybrid paradigms converge to statistically equivalent accuracy
  (R^2 approx 0.55, TOST-equivalent at Delta=0.03) despite sharing as
  little as CKA=0.41 representational similarity, extending the platonic
  representation hypothesis to continuous geometric targets. Results
  validated across fourteen backbones on head pose, rigid objects, gaze,
  and camera intrinsics.
